  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: PatPre_2
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: "Since your diagnosis, research has become a source of hope for you.
    Your cancer center offers monthly educational sessions for patients, and this
    month you\u2019re learning about the use of patient samples in early research
    \u2014 before trials. During the session, you overhear someone say, \u201CIn
    lab-based studies, researchers usually choose the most common or convenient animal
    or human cells available, because all cell types can give equally useful information
    about a disease.\u201D"
  explanation: "Not all cells provide the same kind of information. Some better reflect
    how a disease behaves in the body, while others may be used for early screening
    because they\u2019re easier or cheaper to work with. Researchers primarily choose
    cells that best represent the disease, while also considering availability and
    cost. Balancing these choices helps focus on treatments with the best chance
    of being safe and effective for people."
  correctAnswer: False
  incorrectAnswer1: True
  incorrectAnswer2: 
  incorrectAnswer3: 
  questionRole: Patient
  questionType: TrueFalse
  questionStage: Preclinical
